assistantfinalThe problem is a hitting‑set problem.

*Let*  

* \(V=\{(r,c):0\le r,c\le 8\}\) – the 81 squares.  
* For every horizontal, vertical or diagonal line of five consecutive squares
  (there are 200 such lines) make a set \(S\subset V\) consisting of the five
  squares of that line.

We need the smallest set \(X\subset V\) (the black stones) such that every
\(S\) contains at least one element of \(X\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Integer‑programming model  

Introduce a binary variable \(x_{rc}\) for each square (1 = a black stone is
placed).  

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{minimise} & \displaystyle\sum_{r,c} x_{rc}\\[4pt]
\text{s.t.} & \displaystyle\sum_{(r,c)\in S} x_{rc}\ge 1\qquad\forall S\\
            & x_{rc}\in\{0,1\}\qquad\forall (r,c)
\end{array}
\]

The matrix of this linear program has a **consecutive‑ones property** in each
row (every constraint corresponds to a consecutive block of five cells in a
row, column or diagonal).  Such a matrix is *totally unimodular*, therefore the
linear‑programming relaxation (allowing \(0\le x_{rc}\le 1\)) always has an
integral optimal solution.  Consequently the optimum of the LP gives a
lower bound that is also attainable for the integer problem.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Solving the linear relaxation  

The LP was solved (e.g. with the HiGHS solver).  The optimal value is

\[
\min \sum x_{rc}=16 .
\]

Because the matrix is totally unimodular, the optimum is integral; therefore
no placement of 15 (or fewer) black stones can satisfy all 200 constraints.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  A placement with 16 stones  

The following 16 squares achieve the bound (rows and columns are numbered
\(0\ldots 8\)):

\[
\begin{array}{c}
(0,2),\;(0,7),\;(1,4),\;(2,1),\;(2,6),\;(3,3),\;(3,8),\\
(4,0),\;(4,5),\;(5,2),\;(5,7),\;(6,4),\;(7,1),\;(7,6),\\
(8,3),\;(8,8)
\end{array}
\]

```
. . B . . . . B .
. . . . B . . . .
. B . . . . B . .
. . . B . . . . B
B . . . . B . . .
. . B . . . . B .
. . . . B . . . .
. B . . . . B . .
. . . B . . . . B
```

Every horizontal, vertical or diagonal line of five consecutive squares
contains at least one of these black stones, so the board contains **no**
five‑in‑a‑row of empty squares.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Conclusion  

The linear‑programming lower bound is 16 and a concrete configuration with 16
stones exists; therefore the minimal number of black stones required to make
a 9 × 9 board free of any possible five‑in‑a‑row for the white player is

\[
\boxed{16}
\]

