assistantanalysisassistantanalysisassistantanalysisI'll output a code block.assistantfinal**Solution Overview**

The grid is a \(60\times 60\) board, i.e. a bipartite graph with the two colour classes  

\[
A=\{\text{black squares}\},\qquad B=\{\text{white squares}\},
\]

\(|A|=|B|=1800\).  
Every edge joins a vertex of \(A\) with a vertex of \(B\).

For a labelling \(f\colon A\cup B\to\{1,\dots ,3600\}\) (each number used once) put  

\[
S(f)=\min_{(u,v)\text{ adjacent}} \bigl(f(u)+f(v)\bigr).
\]

We have to maximise \(S(f)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  A necessary upper bound

Let  

\[
a_{\min}= \min_{a\in A} f(a),\qquad
b_{\min}= \min_{b\in B} f(b).
\]

Because every edge contains one vertex of each colour,

\[
S(f)\le a_{\min}+b_{\min}. \tag{1}
\]

The global minimum of the whole board is \(1\); it belongs either to \(A\) or to \(B\).  
Assume without loss of generality that \(1\in A\). Then \(a_{\min}=1\) and (1) gives

\[
S(f)\le 1+b_{\min}.
\]

The set \(B\) contains exactly \(1800\) numbers.  
If we want \(b_{\min}\) as large as possible we must put the \(1800\) largest numbers on \(B\); then  

\[
b_{\min}=1801 .
\]

Consequently

\[
S(f)\le 1+1801 = 1802 . \tag{2}
\]

No labelling can give a larger value of \(S\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Showing the bound is attainable

Place the numbers \(1,2,\dots ,1800\) on the black squares and the numbers
\(1801,1802,\dots ,3600\) on the white squares (any bijection between the
two colour classes works).  

*Every black square is adjacent only to white squares, whose values are at
least \(1801\).*  
Hence for any adjacent pair \((\text{black},\text{white})\)

\[
\text{sum}\ge 1+1801 = 1802 .
\]

Thus the constructed labelling satisfies \(S(f)=1802\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Optimality

From (2) we have the universal upper bound \(S\le 1802\).  
From the construction we have a concrete labelling attaining \(S=1802\).  
Therefore the maximum possible value of the minimal adjacent sum is exactly

\[
\boxed{1802}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

**Answer:** \(\displaystyle 1802\).

