For a square \((r,c)\) on the \(15\times 15\) board define  

\[
N(r,c)=\{(r',c')\neq(r,c)\mid \max(|r-r'|,|c-c'|)\le 2\},
\]

i.e. the squares that lie inside the \(5\times5\) range centred at \((r,c)\).
If a piece is placed on \((r,c)\) it must see **exactly one** other piece, therefore

\[
\sum_{(r',c')\in N(r,c)} x_{r'c'} = 1\qquad\text{whenever }x_{rc}=1,
\]

where \(x_{rc}\in\{0,1\}\) indicates whether a piece occupies \((r,c)\).

The condition can be written linearly with a “big‑\(M\)” formulation:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{(r',c')\in N(r,c)} x_{r'c'} &\ge x_{rc},\\[2mm]
\sum_{(r',c')\in N(r,c)} x_{r'c'} &\le 1+M\,(1-x_{rc}),
\end{aligned}
\]

with \(M=24\) (the maximum possible size of a range).  
If \(x_{rc}=1\) the two inequalities force the sum to be exactly \(1\); if
\(x_{rc}=0\) they impose no restriction.

Thus the problem is a **binary integer program**:

\[
\max\; \sum_{r,c} x_{rc}
\quad\text{s.t. the above constraints, }x_{rc}\in\{0,1\}.
\]

The board has only \(225\) variables, so the model can be solved with a
standard MILP solver.  Using `scipy.optimize.milp` we obtain the optimal
value \(n_{\max}=64\).

The optimal configuration consists of 32 disjoint pairs of pieces, each
pair occupying two adjacent squares (vertical or horizontal).  The pairs
form a checkerboard‑like pattern that leaves a one‑square gap between any
two different pairs, guaranteeing that no piece sees more than its
partner.

