If we use diagonal separation (dx=1, dy=1) (adjacent diagonally), the union shape is maybe 5x5 square (since squares overlap heavily). Actually centers distance sqrt(2) but Chebyshev distance=1. The two 5x5 squares overlap heavily; union extents: x from i-2 to i+1+2 = i+3? Wait left piece at (i,j), right piece at (i+1,j+1). Their x ranges: left piece [i-2,i+2]; right piece [i-1,i+3]; union x: i-2 to i+3 (size 6). y ranges: left [j-2,j+2]; right [j-1,j+3]; union y: j-2 to j+3 (size 6). So union is 6x6 rectangle.

Thus different pair configurations result in different forbidden region shapes.

Goal: maximize number of pieces (edges) we can place on board while respecting that forbidden rectangles of different pairs do not intersect (including overlapping with pieces). Actually they can intersect as long as they don't contain any piece from other pair, but since they are rectangles of forbidden zones (distance ≤2 from a piece), any overlap would cause some cell that is within distance ≤2 of a piece in each pair; but if there is no piece in the overlapping area (just empty cells), it's okay. However, if the forbidden zones intersect, could there be a piece from pair A that lies within distance ≤2 of piece from pair B? That would be a conflict, but that would mean that the zones intersect at that piece location. Since each piece is at the center of its own zone, any overlap that includes a piece location would cause conflict. Overlap that does not include piece locations may be allowed. However, to guarantee no conflict, we must ensure that the distance between any two pieces from different pairs is ≥3. That's equivalent to ensuring the Chebyshev distance between any two points from distinct pairs is ≥3. This is a simpler condition to enforce.

